{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"$\\cos(x)x^3$ is the product of two, more basic, expressions: $\\cos(x)$ and $x^3$.\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ can be found using **the product rule**:\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{d}{dx}[u(x)v(x)]\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}[u(x)]v(x)+u(x)\\dfrac{d}{dx}[v(x)]\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=u'(x)v(x)+u(x)v'(x)\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[\\cos(x)x^3\\right]\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[\\cos(x)\\right]x^3+\\cos(x)\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^3]\u0026\u0026\\gray{\\text{The product rule}}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-\\sin(x)x^3+\\cos(x)3x^2\u0026\u0026\\gray{\\text{Differentiate }\\cos(x)\\text{ and }x^3}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=3x^2\\cos(x)-x^3\\sin(x)\u0026\u0026\\gray{\\text{Simplify}}\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion,\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=3x^2\\cos(x)-x^3\\sin(x)$\n\nor any other equivalent form.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"Let $y=\\cos(x)x^3$.\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=$ [[☃ expression 1]]\n\n","images":{},"widgets":{"expression 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answerForms":[{"considered":"correct","form":false,"key":0,"simplify":false,"value":"3x^2\\cos(x)-x^3\\sin(x)"}],"buttonSets":["basic","trig","prealgebra"],"functions":["f","g","h"],"times":false},"static":false,"type":"expression","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
